1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Start with stock prices, volume and records of options trading.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Build price-history, option-state and activity features from eligible records.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Fit log ridge and LightGBM; compare their variance forecasts with observed outcomes.

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:36,000
FMP supplies one-minute stock bars: asset, time, close price and traded volume.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Unusual Whales supplies options-trade records with contract details and attached
quotes.

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Both sources are matched by underlying stock and time before features are built.

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Apple, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Nvidia and Tesla are the stocks studied; SPY and QQQ
are controls.

8
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:05,000
At a 10:00 forecast, the source-time cutoff is 09:58: two minutes earlier.

9
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
An earlier price bar or option record can enter only if it also passes quality
checks.

10
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:21,000
A zero-size trade is rejected even before the cutoff; a later quote is too late for
this forecast.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:29,000
We filter individual records, not entire variables. Source time is an availability
proxy.

12
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:38,000
These are sixteen observed Apple closes. The price falls from $332.88 to $330.27,
with intermediate rebounds.

13
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Each pair of adjacent closes produces one log return; a fall gives a negative return.

14
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Squaring removes the sign. Larger price moves contribute more to the variance sum.

15
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:05,000
This past fifteen-minute component joins other history windows, volume, SPY/QQQ and
session timing.

16
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Locate the contract by days to expiry and strike divided by spot.

17
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Within its cell, the size-weighted median summarizes implied volatility.

18
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Count qualifying trades, sum their size and premium, and summarize side-tag
composition.

19
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Match the same contract across records to measure changes in IV, midpoint and spread.

20
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Exposure proxies use calculated sensitivities; open interest is a separate snapshot
input.

21
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:11,000
The same asset, session and origin identify each row; every forecast targets the next
15 minutes.

22
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Each larger set retains the preceding inputs. Compare forecasts at matching eligible
origins.

23
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Log the inputs, then use training means and standard deviations.

24
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Choose regularization on past validation sessions; refit on the permitted past.

25
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Multiply standardized inputs by fitted weights, then add the intercept.

26
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Use training residuals to correct the exponential, then apply training bounds.

27
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Tree 1 tests past variance, then implied volatility; the highlighted path selects a
leaf.

28
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Other trees test the same row and add their own learned contributions in log-variance
units.

29
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Add the contributions to the base, then exponentiate to obtain the RV15 variance
forecast.

30
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Training expands by session after an initial 60-session warm-up.

31
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:47,000
The last ten training sessions are used for internal selection.

32
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:54,000
A 60-minute purge and embargo separate potentially overlapping windows.

33
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,000
The model is refitted for each session without using future test outcomes.

34
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Keep the forecast fixed before revealing the target observations. This is a new Apple
window.

35
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Observed closes arrive in order; each new return adds its square to the running
variance sum.

36
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Downward moves and upward moves both add variance. An unchanged close adds zero.

37
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:37,000
The completed RV15 is the observed outcome used to score forecasts; it is not a model
prediction.

38
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Subtract losses at matching forecast times: price history minus added option state.

39
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Average minutes within each company and day, then give companies equal weight.

40
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Resample five-session blocks to assess uncertainty in the mean loss improvement.

41
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Test option state first; test added activity only if that first test passes.

42
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Subtract forecast variance from observed variance at the same two forecast times.

43
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:27,000
MAE averages absolute errors; it describes their typical size.

44
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:34,000
RMSE squares errors before averaging, giving more weight to large errors.

45
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:41,000
These describe errors in variance units; QLIKE drives the primary comparison.

46
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:48,500
Match the company, session, forecast minute and fifteen-minute horizon.

47
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:56,000
Each information set predicts the same future realised variance.

48
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:03,500
QLIKE compares observed variance with forecast variance; lower is better.

49
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:11,000
Repeat this comparison separately for log ridge and LightGBM.

50
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:18,000
The primary historical study retains its original data, eligibility and saved
results.

51
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Feature repairs and robustness checks remain distinct from the primary results.

52
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Additional model families were compared in a separate historical analysis.

53
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:39,000
ETF forecasting is a separate extension; none rewrites the original study.

54
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,500
A local variant studies options activity without the IV filter, under a separate
scope.

55
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:52,000
The expanded design combines Massive features with Greeks from UW.

56
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,500
Massive covers a selected basket of up to twelve contracts, not the full chain.

57
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:05,000
These historical extensions have separate protocols; they preserve the primary study.

58
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Start with price bars and option records, applying the source-time cutoff.

59
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:19,000
Build price-history, option-state and activity features from eligible observations.

60
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:26,000
Log ridge and LightGBM forecast variance; QLIKE, MAE and RMSE score the errors.

61
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:33,000
Compare matched forecasts by session and quantify uncertainty in the improvement.
